# [M6.4.3] D27 blinded corpus -- atomic-groups module.
#
# Genuine PCRE2 compile-time rejections involving possessive-quantifier
# syntax or atomic-group syntax -- every pattern below was confirmed to
# fail `pcre2_compile` on libpcre2 10.46 (see README.md for the command),
# so a correct implementation must refuse every one of these, never
# miscompile them (CLAUDE.md's "never miscompile" mandate).
#
# Every block carries `features atomic-groups`, so this file tests
# rejection of genuinely malformed syntax WITH the module enabled --
# distinct from gating.rxt, which tests the module-DISABLED refusal
# direction on otherwise-VALID syntax. Conflating the two would let an
# implementation that only ever checks "is the module enabled" (and never
# validates the syntax itself) pass this file by accident.
#
# A note on what could be measured pre-implementation (build/pcrec today
# refuses every atomic/possessive construct outright, per the cell brief):
# `pcrec` ALREADY independently rejects several of these for the SAME
# reason PCRE2 does, via checks that have nothing to do with the
# atomic-groups module gate and fire identically with or without
# `--features atomic-groups` -- confirmed directly against the CLI, not
# assumed: `^*+`/`^++` (quantified bare anchor -- "quantifier does not
# follow a repeatable item"), `a{2,1}+` (numbers out of order), `a*?+`/
# `a??+` (multiple quantifiers on the same item), and `a{65536}+` (number
# too big -- U5's existing ceiling). The rest (`(?>a`, `(?>a))`, `(?>*a)`,
# `a+++`, `a?+?`, `a*++`, `a++?`) currently reach only the module-gate
# refusal ("possessive quantifier requires module 'atomic-groups'" or the
# `(?>...)` equivalent) because pcrec cannot look past "is this construct
# implemented yet" -- these are a forward promise, checked here only on
# exit code (perr, per docs/testing.md, never asserts message text), to
# be genuinely exercised once the module lands.

# Two quantifiers stacked on one item with no possessive marker involved
# at all -- PCRE2 rejects this regardless of atomic-groups.
pattern a*?+
features atomic-groups
perr

pattern a*++
features atomic-groups
perr

pattern a++?
features atomic-groups
perr

pattern a??+
features atomic-groups
perr

pattern a?+?
features atomic-groups
perr

# A THIRD quantifier marker with nothing left to attach to.
pattern a+++
features atomic-groups
perr

# Unclosed atomic group.
pattern (?>a
features atomic-groups
perr

# Unmatched closing parenthesis after an otherwise-well-formed atomic
# group.
pattern (?>a))
features atomic-groups
perr

# A bare `*` with no repeatable item before it, inside an atomic group.
pattern (?>*a)
features atomic-groups
perr

# A possessive marker directly on an anchor -- PCRE2 rejects quantifying
# a bare anchor at all (independent of atomic-groups), so the possessive
# spelling is rejected the same way the plain quantified-anchor form is.
pattern ^*+
features atomic-groups
perr

pattern ^++
features atomic-groups
perr

# Out-of-order bounds, still rejected the same way with a possessive
# suffix as without one.
pattern a{2,1}+
features atomic-groups
perr

# Repeat count above PCRE2's 65535 ceiling (U5), still rejected with a
# possessive suffix.
pattern a{65536}+
features atomic-groups
perr
